  "query_preview": "..\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Soaking rain is expected areawide tonight into \nThursday AM to help with ongoing drought conditions.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 1 DESCRIPTION... \nA broad low pressure system in northern Canada will bring a \nstrong warm front through Maine tonight, with the associated \noccluded front quickly exiting the region early Thursday AM. \nAnticipating rainfall to move in from the west tonight, \nbecoming areawide and moderate after midnight. Cannot fully \nrule out am embedded isolated thunderstorm, but not \nanticipating severe weather given very marginal instability. \n\nAs of this update, rainfall totals should reach between \n0.5 to 0.75 inches throughout the region. Hi-res CAMs have\nallowed for probabilities to increase for 1 inch of rain along\nthe Downeast coast extending inland to The Airline. The trend\ncontinues for the least amount of rain to fall in the\nQuebec/Maine borderlands and St. John Valley where only 0.25 to\n0.5 inch expected. Localized higher totals are possible with \nheavier convection that develops overnight. Flooding is not a \nconcern with this system, but rain amounts should give some \nrelief to the ongoing D1-D2 drought conditions. As the system \nexits on Thursday, some wrap around moisture from the system \ncould bring some isolated showers through the north.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Strong gusty winds expected with the rain late \ntonight and again during the day Thursday and Friday. Cannot \nrule out isolated power outages.\n\nKEY MESSAGE 2 DESCRIPTION... As mentioned above, strong low \npressure system passes to the north of the forecast area, with \nstrong winds aloft. This will bring strong gusts late tonight \nin the Downeast region, and again during the day Thursday from \nthe west, and again from the west during the day Friday. The \nwinds aloft will be the strongest tonight, with up to about \n50-60 kt at 850mb, but there will be lower surface gusts due to\nstable surface layer. More realistically, we are looking at \ngusts as high as about 30 mph from the south tonight, with \ngusts potentially up to 35 mph. Low level jet develops late tonight,\nbringing strong gusts over the waters. Gusts around 35 mph from\nthe west appear likely Thursday, and with wet soils after \ntonights rain, plus leaves on the trees, there could be very \nisolated tree damage/power outages. Wind gusts gradually relax \nThursday evening, but pick back up on Friday with gusts up to 35\nmph again possible, mainly over the north.",
